  "problem": "Find the value of $x\\in (-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The relative maximum is at $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=3$ only.",
